From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: W4HO <87742609+W4HO-ham@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 05:14:55 -0647
Subject: [PATCH] nfc: stop polling before taking the device down on an rfkill
 block

nfc_rfkill_set_block() calls nfc_dev_down(), which refuses a device that
is polling or has an active target with -EBUSY, and the return value is
ignored. Userspace such as neard keeps a poll loop running whenever the
adapter is powered, so blocking NFC through rfkill left the radio on and
polling.

Deactivate the active target and stop polling first, then take the
device down. This stays in the rfkill path rather than in
nfc_dev_down(): when userspace asks for DEV_DOWN itself, the -EBUSY
tells it to stop its own poll first, while an rfkill block has no one
to tell.

After a stop from here, genl_data.poll_req_portid still names the
process that started the poll. Its later STOP_POLL returns -EINVAL at
the !dev->polling check and its next START_POLL sets the id again, so
nothing breaks.

On a Pixel 9a (ST54L on nxp-nci, 7.3.0-rc6-omamobile-tegu-s2mpg15-m1,
net/nfc/core.c as in this patch) with neard polling, `rfkill block nfc`
left the controller interrupting about 14 times a second; with this
change the interrupts stop and nfctool reports the device unpowered.

Fixes: be055b2f89b5 ("NFC: RFKILL support")
Cc: stable@vger.kernel.org
Signed-off-by: W4HO <87742609+W4HO-ham@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 net/nfc/core.c | 15 +++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 15 insertions(+)

diff --git a/net/nfc/core.c b/net/nfc/core.c
index f52166929..56a3cf684 100644
--- a/net/nfc/core.c
+++ b/net/nfc/core.c
@@ -170,12 +170,27 @@ int nfc_dev_down(struct nfc_dev *dev)
 static int nfc_rfkill_set_block(void *data, bool blocked)
 {
 	struct nfc_dev *dev = data;
+	struct nfc_target *target;
+	u32 target_idx;
 
 	pr_debug("%s blocked %d", dev_name(&dev->dev), blocked);
 
 	if (!blocked)
 		return 0;
 
+	/*
+	 * nfc_dev_down() refuses a device that polls or has an active
+	 * target, so a block during discovery left the radio on: stop both
+	 * first.
+	 */
+	device_lock(&dev->dev);
+	target = dev->active_target;
+	target_idx = target ? target->idx : 0;
+	device_unlock(&dev->dev);
+	if (target)
+		nfc_deactivate_target(dev, target_idx, NFC_TARGET_MODE_IDLE);
+	nfc_stop_poll(dev);
+
 	nfc_dev_down(dev);
 
 	return 0;
